<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Desk | Food Supply Crisis Report"
  description="Learn how Food Supply Crisis Report articles are authored, sourced, dated, updated and corrected."
  showAds={false}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Trust &amp; standards</p>
      <h1>Food Supply Crisis Report Editorial Desk</h1>
      <p>The Editorial Desk prepares evidence-led explainers, analysis and current reporting for readers who want context without panic.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <h2>Authorship</h2>
      <p>Articles are published under the <strong>Food Supply Crisis Report Editorial Desk</strong> byline. This is an editorial byline for the publication rather than a claim that every article was written by a single named person.</p>

      <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
      <p>We prefer primary and authoritative material for important factual claims. Examples include FAO and WFP publications, IPC assessments, national statistical offices, agriculture ministries, central banks where relevant to food prices, climate agencies, and original datasets or research.</p>
      <p>When a claim comes from a third party, the article should attribute it clearly. Source links are retained in the article so readers can review the original material.</p>

      <h2>Facts, forecasts and scenarios</h2>
      <p>We distinguish between observed data, official estimates, reported events, forecasts and analytical scenarios. A possible future disruption is not treated as a confirmed shortage. Country-specific hunger or famine risk is kept geographically and temporally specific rather than generalized to the entire world.</p>

      <h2>Dates and updates</h2>
      <p>Articles display publication dates and, when material changes are made, an updated date. Time-sensitive claims should be read with their source date in mind because food prices, harvest estimates, shipping conditions and humanitarian assessments can change quickly.</p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>We welcome evidence-based corrections. Readers should identify the article, the exact claim, why it is incorrect or incomplete, and a credible source supporting the correction. Material factual corrections are prioritized over stylistic suggestions.</p>

      <h2>What we avoid</h2>
      <p>We avoid fabricated certainty, fear-driven headlines, unsupported claims of global collapse, duplicate pages created only for search traffic and advice that could encourage panic buying or unsafe storage practices.</p>

      <p>For the full publication standard, see our <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>. For correction requests, use <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
